        <section id="pure-and-impure-functions"  class="t-bg-primary">
          
            <h2>Pure and impure functions</h2>
              <p>The capabilities, restrictions, and efficiency of programs depends on how they are written. Using different paradigms and coding styles, we can incorporate specific characteristics that we want from our program. For example, by using pure functions, it is easier to achieve parallelisation. Here we discuss ‘pure’ and ‘impure’ functions, and their benefits.</p>

              
              <!-- IF 2 --><ul class="l-icon-grid ">
  
  
    
    <!--  -->
      
  
  
    
  
  <li class="c-reveal-block">
    <img class="c-reveal-block__icon js-showhide" src="~/images/svg/uc/uc_u3_s3_p1_reveal1.svg" alt="Pure functions" width="266" height="266">
    
    <div class="c-reveal-block__desc">
      <h3 aria-hidden="true">Pure functions</h3>
      <ol>
<li>They always give the same return value for a given input</li>
<li>They do not change anything unrelated to the function (no side effects)</li>
<li>They do not modify any arguments which are passed to them</li>
</ol>

    </div>
  </li>
      
  
  
    
  
  <li class="c-reveal-block">
    <img class="c-reveal-block__icon js-showhide" src="~/images/svg/uc/uc_u3_s3_p1_reveal2.svg" alt="Impure functions" width="266" height="266">
    
    <div class="c-reveal-block__desc">
      <h3 aria-hidden="true">Impure functions</h3>
      <p>An impure function is essentially a function that violates the criteria for a pure function. For example, this could be a function which changes a variable which is being held in the memory OUTSIDE of a function call which is not one of the output variables.</p>

    </div>
  </li>
  
</ul>

            
          
        </section>
        
    
     
    
  
     
    
  
  
