        <section id="potential-dependencies-/-needs-in-pure-function" >
          
            <h2>Potential dependencies / needs in pure function</h2>
              <p>Pure functions are not dependent on global variables. The output only depends on the input values, and the computation that occurs INSIDE the function. They hold no internal state between calls. This guarantees that a given function ALWAYS returns the same results for a given set of inputs.</p>
<p>Pure functions have no side effects. They also do not change the input variable(s). They therefore only have local effects (e.g. whatever action the function is called to perform), or only change the output variables.</p>
<p>If the function is dependent on a global variable that changes between function calls, the exact same inputs could return a different value. This violates our rule 1 above (same input for same output), as is illustrated in the following Python code:</p>

              
              <div class="c-code"><pre class="language-python"><code class="language-python"><span class="highlight-line"><span class="token comment"># Define a pure function</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line"><span class="token keyword">def</span> <span class="token function">pure_function</span><span class="token punctuation">(</span>input_a<span class="token punctuation">,</span> input_b<span class="token punctuation">)</span><span class="token punctuation">:</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line">    <span class="token comment"># Define a local variable (cannot be changed outside of function)</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line">    loc_x <span class="token operator">=</span> <span class="token number">3</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line">    output <span class="token operator">=</span> <span class="token punctuation">(</span><span class="token punctuation">(</span>input_a<span class="token operator">/</span>input_b<span class="token punctuation">)</span> <span class="token operator">+</span> loc_x<span class="token punctuation">)</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line">    <span class="token keyword">print</span><span class="token punctuation">(</span><span class="token string">'Pure output ='</span><span class="token punctuation">,</span> output<span class="token punctuation">)</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line">    <span class="token keyword">return</span> </span><br><span class="highlight-line"></span><br><span class="highlight-line"><span class="token comment"># Call the pure function</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line">pure_function<span class="token punctuation">(</span><span class="token number">8</span><span class="token punctuation">,</span> <span class="token number">2</span><span class="token punctuation">)</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line"></span><br><span class="highlight-line"><span class="token comment"># Define an impure function</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line"><span class="token keyword">def</span> <span class="token function">impure_function</span><span class="token punctuation">(</span>input_a<span class="token punctuation">,</span> input_b<span class="token punctuation">)</span><span class="token punctuation">:</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line">  </span><br><span class="highlight-line">    <span class="token comment"># The global variable defined below is called here</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line">    <span class="token keyword">global</span> glob_x</span><br><span class="highlight-line">    </span><br><span class="highlight-line">    output <span class="token operator">=</span> <span class="token punctuation">(</span><span class="token punctuation">(</span>input_a<span class="token operator">/</span>input_b<span class="token punctuation">)</span> <span class="token operator">+</span> glob_x<span class="token punctuation">)</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line">  <span class="token keyword">print</span><span class="token punctuation">(</span><span class="token string">'Impure output ='</span><span class="token punctuation">,</span> output<span class="token punctuation">)</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line">  <span class="token keyword">return</span> </span><br><span class="highlight-line"></span><br><span class="highlight-line"><span class="token comment"># Define a global variable then set its value</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line"><span class="token keyword">global</span> glob_x</span><br><span class="highlight-line">glob_x <span class="token operator">=</span> <span class="token number">3</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line"></span><br><span class="highlight-line"><span class="token comment"># Call the impure function</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line">impure_function<span class="token punctuation">(</span><span class="token number">8</span><span class="token punctuation">,</span> <span class="token number">2</span><span class="token punctuation">)</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line"></span><br><span class="highlight-line"><span class="token comment"># Change the value of the global variable</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line">glob_x <span class="token operator">=</span> <span class="token number">6</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line"></span><br><span class="highlight-line"><span class="token comment"># Then call the impure function again with exactly the same inputs</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line">impure_function<span class="token punctuation">(</span><span class="token number">8</span><span class="token punctuation">,</span> <span class="token number">2</span><span class="token punctuation">)</span></span></code></pre>
</div>

            
          
        </section>
        
    
     
    
  
  
        <section id="result" >
          
            <h2>Result</h2>
              
              <div class="c-code"><pre class="language-text"><code class="language-text"><span class="highlight-line">Pure output = 7.0</span><br><span class="highlight-line">Impure output = 7.0</span><br><span class="highlight-line">Impure output = 10.0</span><br><span class="highlight-line">[Finished in 139ms]</span></code></pre>
</div>

            
              <p>Note: in Python, indents at the start of the line indicate blocks of codes. The indents below the line in which the function is defined using ‘def’ indicate that they are within the function. The code runs from the top to the bottom.</p>
<p>With exactly the same inputs, the impure function gives a different result the second time.</p>
<p>This is because the calculation in our impure function is dependent on the global variable 'glob_x', which can be changed EXTERNALLY to the function.</p>
<p>However, in our pure function, the factor ‘loc_x’ is determined INTERNALLY in the function. It therefore cannot be changed from outside.</p>

          
        </section>
        
    
     
    
  
     
    
  
  
